    [movie_title] => Go  Goa Gone
    [description] => Go Goa Gone is a 2013 Indian Hindi-language zombie action comedy film directed by Raj and D.K. The film features Saif Ali Khan, Kunal Khemu, Vir Das, Anand Tiwari and Puja Gupta. It was released on 10 May 2013.[3] Almost one third of the film was shot in Mauritius.[4] It is the first Indian zombie film and has also been described as India's first stoner film. Go Goa Gone was successful at the box office. It received mixed reviews, but it received praise for its pacing, editing, and use of zombies, though the screenplay and the second act received criticism. The film became a cult film over the years. A sequel titled Go Goa Gone 2 was announced, but as of 2024, the film is not in production.
    [dest_1] => Dona Paula Jetty
    [description1] => Dona Paula is a popular tourist spot in Goa, known for its scenic beauty, romantic legends, and stunning sea views. Located near Panaji, this picturesque spot offers breathtaking views of the Arabian Sea and is famous for the Dona Paula Jetty and the National Institute of Oceanography. The place is named after Dona Paula de Menezes, a Portuguese noblewoman, and has a romantic legend associated with it, making it a favorite among couples. Visitors can enjoy water sports, visit the nearby Miramar Beach, or explore local markets. The sunset views from Dona Paula are especially mesmerizing.
    [city1] => Panaji
    [states1] => Goa
    [dest_2] => Arpora Market
    [description2] => Arpora Market, also known as the Saturday Night Market, is one of Goa’s most vibrant shopping and entertainment hubs. Located in Arpora, near Baga and Anjuna, this market comes alive every Saturday evening with a mix of stalls selling handicrafts, jewelry, clothing, souvenirs, and local Goan delicacies. It's also famous for live music, street performances, and a variety of food stalls offering global cuisines. The market has a lively, bohemian vibe, attracting both tourists and locals. Open from November to April, it’s a must-visit for those looking to experience Goa’s nightlife beyond beaches and clubs.
    [city2] => North Goa
    [states2] => Goa
    [dest_3] => Parra Road
    [description3] => Parra, a picturesque village in North Goa, is famous for its scenic beauty and iconic coconut tree-lined road, which gained popularity through Bollywood movies and social media. Located near Mapusa and close to popular beaches like Anjuna and Baga, Parra offers a peaceful escape from Goa’s busy tourist hubs. The village is known for its lush green fields, traditional Goan houses, and serene countryside vibes. It’s a great spot for photography, cycling, and experiencing Goa’s local charm. If you’re looking for a quiet, scenic drive, Parra’s palm-fringed road is a must-visit!
    [city3] => Parra
    [states3] => Goa
    [dest_4] => Goa City
    [description4] => Goa, India’s smallest state, is a tropical paradise known for its stunning beaches, vibrant nightlife, Portuguese heritage, and laid-back vibe. Popular beaches like Baga, Anjuna, and Palolem offer water sports, beach shacks, and breathtaking sunsets. The state is rich in history, with attractions like the Basilica of Bom Jesus, Aguada Fort, and Chapora Fort. Adventure seekers can explore Dudhsagar Waterfalls, spice plantations, and wildlife sanctuaries. Goa’s lively markets, seafood-rich cuisine, and nightlife make it a favorite among travelers. Best visited between November and February, it’s easily accessible by air, rail, and road, offering the perfect mix of relaxation and adventure.
    [city4] => Goa
    [states4] => Goa
    [budget] => 6000
